      "index": "b05",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2121121268,
      "version": 258,
      "versionNonce": 1032674164,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793025848850,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NOTES",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NOTES",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "bMDv_LBQt0GtqrLIbadx1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 219.89869560351548,
      "y": 749.5,
      "width": 814.1593627929688,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b06",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1782194164,
      "version": 2275,
      "versionNonce": 1996520308,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793026850829,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A processing block = one fetch/dispatch unit + a group of cores that obey it.\nAn SM has several.\n\nA whole warp is assigned to one processing block; its 32 threads always get\nthe same instruction.\n\nEach thread has its own registers, so the same instructions works on different\nnumbers.\n\nWhy: one fetch/dispatch serves 32 threads, so most of the chip can be cores\ninstead of control.\n\n8 cores and 32 threads: the warp runs in 4 rounds of 8. Still one instruction.\n\nZooming into an SM: the \"control\" and \"cores\" strips are really N processing\nblocks side by side, each one its own fetch/dispatch unit plus\n a group of cores. The memory strip stays shared by the whole SM. A warp\nis assigned to one processing block and runs on its own cores under\nits dispatcher.\n\nGPU runs the grid -> SM runs blocks -> processing blocks run warps -> cores\nrun threads.\n\nBecause CUDA thread scheduling mechanism maps warps to the processing\nblocks and effectively restricts behavior of a warp to execute\nthe same instruction at any point in time, the exeuction behavior\nof a warp is often referred to as Single Instruction Multiple Thread (SIMT).\n\nThe advantage of SIMD is that the cost of the control hardware, such as the \ninstruction fetch/dispatch unit, is shared across many execution units. This design\nchoice allows for a smaller percentage of the hardware to be dedicated\nto control and a larger percentage to be dedicated for increasing\narithmetic throughput. \n\nBecause threads in a warp have a special relationship in the way that they\nare scheduled, there are warp-level primitives. These can be used to improve the\nspeed and efficiency of computations that involved the collaboration between\nthreads.\n\nThese warp-level primitives allow for a warp-centric programming style,\nwhich treats warps as an extra level of the software hierarchy between blocks\nand threads.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A processing block = one fetch/dispatch unit + a group of cores that obey it.\nAn SM has several.\n\nA whole warp is assigned to one processing block; its 32 threads always get\nthe same instruction.\n\nEach thread has its own registers, so the same instructions works on different\nnumbers.\n\nWhy: one fetch/dispatch serves 32 threads, so most of the chip can be cores\ninstead of control.\n\n8 cores and 32 threads: the warp runs in 4 rounds of 8. Still one instruction.\n\nZooming into an SM: the \"control\" and \"cores\" strips are really N processing\nblocks side by side, each one its own fetch/dispatch unit plus\n a group of cores. The memory strip stays shared by the whole SM. A warp\nis assigned to one processing block and runs on its own cores under\nits dispatcher.\n\nGPU runs the grid -> SM runs blocks -> processing blocks run warps -> cores\nrun threads.\n\nBecause CUDA thread scheduling mechanism maps warps to the processing\nblocks and effectively restricts behavior of a warp to execute\nthe same instruction at any point in time, the exeuction behavior\nof a warp is often referred to as Single Instruction Multiple Thread (SIMT).\n\nThe advantage of SIMD is that the cost of the control hardware, such as the \ninstruction fetch/dispatch unit, is shared across many execution units. This design\nchoice allows for a smaller percentage of the hardware to be dedicated\nto control and a larger percentage to be dedicated for increasing\narithmetic throughput. \n\nBecause threads in a warp have a special relationship in the way that they\nare scheduled, there are warp-level primitives. These can be used to improve the\nspeed and efficiency of computations that involved the collaboration between\nthreads.\n\nThese warp-level primitives allow for a warp-centric programming style,\nwhich treats warps as an extra level of the software hierarchy between blocks\nand threads.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_7a_a3e2OJxk-3iC6RFlx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040.2896984018419,
      "y": 198.66972541114475,
      "width": 317.19976806640625,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 883550540,
      "version": 126,
      "versionNonce": 1702468812,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793027316854,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The number of processing blocks\non the SM depends on the GPU \ngeneration.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The number of processing blocks\non the SM depends on the GPU \ngeneration.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HBCe2k24pLH2wtXfrQXRB",
